def find_roots(
    graph: "Graph", prop: "URIRef", roots: Optional[Set["Node"]] = None
) -> Set["Node"]:
    """Find the roots in some sort of transitive hierarchy."""
    # Initialize roots set if not provided
    if roots is None:
        roots = set()
        
    # Get all subjects and objects involved in the property relationship
    non_roots = set()
    for _subject, _object in graph.subject_objects(prop):
        # Add all objects (parents) to roots set
        roots.add(_object)
        # Add all subjects (children) to non_roots set
        non_roots.add(_subject)
    
    # If no explicit roots were found, use all subjects as potential roots
    if not roots:
        for _subject, _object in graph.subject_objects(prop):
            roots.add(_subject)
            roots.add(_object)
    
    # Remove all non-root nodes (nodes that have parents)
    roots -= non_roots
    
    return roots
</repaired_code>

The fixed implementation:
1. Initializes an empty set of roots if none provided
2. Tracks non-root nodes (those that have parents) in non_roots set
3. Collects all nodes involved in the property relationship
4. Removes nodes that have parents (non-roots) from the roots set
5. Returns the final set of root nodes

This implementation properly handles the transitive hierarchy by identifying nodes that don't have any parents for the given property relationship.

